WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:07.509
We now have some data with which we can evaluate
what Donald Trump's tariffs, which are part

00:00:07.509 --> 00:00:10.130
of his trade war, have done to the economy.

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:16.490
Remember the whole point of putting tariffs
on goods from other countries is to make those

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:23.439
goods more expensive so that American people
and businesses will buy local as it were,

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:26.050
switching to American goods from foreign goods.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.670
That's the idea.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:34.160
The problem is that we depend on cheap foreign
stuff from countries like China, much more

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.160
than China.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.160
Depends on us.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.590
So when both countries tariff each other,
which is what we've seen happen, our stuff

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.550
gets more expensive.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:48.300
While China gets stuff from other countries,
to put it simply, this is a vast simplification,

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.680
but we're just trying to get caught up so
we can have today's conversation.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:56.750
The federal reserve, which is run of course
by Donald Trump's fed chairman, has put together

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:57.750
a report.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:03.359
And what we learned from that report is that
Donald Trump's tariffs led to job losses while

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:05.810
at the same time increasing prices.

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:12.960
Now, if you understand even a little bit about
how tariffs work, this is not a surprise from

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:13.960
day one.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:15.510
We've been saying this is what's going to
happen.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:19.210
But alas, a lot of Donald Trump's supporters
denied that this would happen.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:22.110
So this all started with steel and aluminum
tariffs.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.050
It's grown to many more goods.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:27.430
The manufacturing sector has been the hardest
hit for sure.

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:32.320
Uh, we've seen, uh, agriculture affected,
it has affected many, many industries.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.230
Now the report does say to be charitable.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:38.330
This has been the effect so far.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:44.840
It's hypothetically conceivable that the impact
will reverse in the longer term.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.760
So far it has killed jobs and raised prices.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.580
Maybe at some point it will reverse, but of
course, why would that happen?

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:52.580
It's an obvious question.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:57.980
There is no coherent explanation for why the
effect would reverse over the longer term.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:03.330
This is the problem with focusing on principles
and ignoring the real world, and I've talked

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:04.580
about this before.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:08.430
This is really important for the left to understand
if we want to do well, although it applies

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:09.670
to everybody.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:15.680
When the tariffs started, you had a lot of
people who were speaking in theoretical economic

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.540
terms, so a theoretical economic argument
would be tariffs work because they make foreign

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.510
goods more expensive.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:28.550
That leads to companies using American goods,
which will stimulate our economy and it'll

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:32.600
be bad for China because we'll be pulling
a whole bunch of business away from them and

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:34.780
putting it into domestic businesses.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:40.150
We should protect our economy with tariffs
would be the theoretical outcome or consequence

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:42.580
or conclusion of that idea.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:48.170
It's a lovely theory if we're starting economies
from scratch, but we're not, we have an economy

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:53.160
in the United States that's based on cheap
stuff from China.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.810
Manufacturing was moved to China years ago
and other countries, because it was decided

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:04.130
implicitly, I don't mean you decided, but
as a country it was decided by our leaders

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:07.630
that we value the cheap stuff from China more.

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:14.090
Uh, then we would be hurt by higher prices
if we were to keep all manufacturing in the

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:15.410
United States.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:20.780
Once that's in process, something like a tariff
is not going to have the theoretical effect

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:23.340
that an economics textbook would suggest.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:28.570
China has more alternatives for their stuff
that they tariffs.

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:30.069
The United States on.

00:03:30.069 --> 00:03:35.740
Our economy is based on getting that cheap
stuff from China.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:38.260
And so then obviously the result is what we're
seeing.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:42.560
We are seeing job losses combined with higher
prices.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:47.950
Even economics professors knew this early
on and they said the theoretical impact of

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:55.209
a tariff is not the same as what the real
world impact will be in 2019 United States

00:03:55.209 --> 00:03:56.870
and 2019 China.

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:02.120
If those two countries get into a trade war,
this was coming a mile away and it ends up

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:08.640
being a tax on the middle class who can't
afford to pay more for stuff in the context

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.840
of stagnant wages and everything else that
we've been discussing.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:12.840
So what's next?

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:13.840
I don't know.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.710
We may see the way the administration has
been running things we may see even more tariffs.

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:24.530
We will see what 2020 brings, but the results
not at all surprising to anyone who's been

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:30.280
paying attention and reminder, this is the
federal reserve chaired by Donald Trump's

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:34.070
pick that has told us this is what is going
on.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:35.070
Let me know what you think.

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:39.760
Let let me know if you're surprised, quite
frankly, because it seems as though our audience

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:41.490
basically understood this.

